              "title": "Modern Java Switch Expressions: Arrow Syntax, Yielding Values & Zero Fallthrough",
              "content": "Recognizing that classic switch statements were verbose, error-prone due to fallthrough, and incapable of directly returning values, the Java platform introduced Switch Expressions as a permanent standard language feature in Java 14 (JEP 361). A switch expression transforms the switch construct from a procedural statement (which merely executes side effects) into a functional expression (which computes and evaluates to a single typed value). Switch expressions introduce the arrow operator ('->') between the case label and the case body, completely eliminating the concept of fallthrough! When execution enters a branch defined by 'case VALUE ->', only the expression or block on the right-hand side of the arrow is executed. No break statement is required, and execution can never accidentally leak into subsequent branches.\n\nFurthermore, switch expressions allow comma-separated case labels, dramatically streamlining multi-value matching. Instead of stacking vertical case statements ('case 1: case 2: case 3:'), an engineer can write 'case 1, 2, 3 -> \"Result\";'. This drastically reduces boilerplate while making business rules immediately readable. Because a switch expression produces a value, it can be directly assigned to a variable, passed as an argument to a method, or returned directly from an enclosing method body. For example: 'String message = switch (statusCode) { case 200 -> \"OK\"; case 404 -> \"Not Found\"; default -> \"Error\"; };'. Notice that because the entire switch expression forms a single variable assignment statement, it must conclude with a terminating semicolon (';') after its closing curly brace.\n\nUnder the hood, the Java compiler handles switch expressions with remarkable elegance. Instead of generating intermediate variables on the stack that require manual assignment, javac compiles the switch expression to place the evaluated result directly onto the JVM operand stack. If the switch is used in a return statement, the value on top of the operand stack is returned immediately via the appropriate return bytecode (ireturn for integers, areturn for reference objects), eliminating unnecessary local variable slots and memory copies. This zero-allocation, register-friendly compilation strategy makes switch expressions just as fast as hand-tuned low-level assembly jump tables, completely outperforming chained ternary operators and dynamic map lookups.\n\nWhen a branch in a switch expression requires multi-line execution\u2014such as performing preliminary validation, logging metrics, or calculating intermediate results\u2014the arrow can point to a full block enclosed in curly braces '{ ... }'. Inside a multi-line switch block, you cannot use the return keyword to produce the value for the switch, because 'return' would immediately exit the entire enclosing method! Instead, Java 14 introduced the yield keyword. The yield statement takes an expression, terminates the switch block, and produces that value as the final evaluated result of the switch expression: 'case 500 -> { logger.error(\"Server crash\"); yield \"Internal Server Error\"; }'. The language designers specifically selected 'yield' as a context-sensitive keyword rather than reusing 'break value' (which was trialed in preview builds) to avoid syntactic ambiguity with labeled breaks in nested loops.\n\nMost importantly, switch expressions enforce strict compiler-level exhaustiveness! If you use a switch expression, the Java compiler verifies that every possible value of the selector type is covered. For primitive integers and strings (which have billions of possible permutations), you MUST provide a default branch; omitting default results in a compile-time error: 'the switch expression does not cover all possible input values'. Additionally, switch expressions eliminate common refactoring bugs: because the compiler treats the entire construct as a single cohesive unit, removing a case label or altering a returned type triggers immediate type-checking errors across all branches simultaneously. For enums, if every declared enum constant is explicitly matched in a case branch, the compiler allows you to omit default, guaranteeing compile-time safety and total branch coverage across your enterprise codebase!",
              "code": "public class SwitchExpressionLab {\n    public enum GatewayProtocol {\n        HTTP_REST, GRPC_PROTOBUF, GRAPHQL, WEBSOCKET, KAFKA_STREAM\n    }\n\n    public static int resolveDefaultPort(GatewayProtocol protocol) {\n        // Modern Switch Expression: Evaluates directly to an int value!\n        return switch (protocol) {\n            case HTTP_REST -> 8080;\n            case GRPC_PROTOBUF -> 50051;\n            case GRAPHQL -> 4000;\n            case WEBSOCKET -> 8081;\n            case KAFKA_STREAM -> {\n                // Multi-line block: uses 'yield' to produce value without exiting method\n                System.out.println(\"[Audit] Allocating high-throughput streaming port for Kafka.\");\n                int basePort = 9090;\n                int sslOffset = 2;\n                yield basePort + sslOffset; // Produces 9092\n            }\n            // Exhaustive: all enum constants are covered, so no default is required!\n        };\n    }\n\n    public static String classifyDayModern(int day) {\n        // Switch expression with comma-separated multi-label matching\n        return switch (day) {\n            case 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 -> \"Weekday\";\n            case 6, 7 -> \"Weekend\";\n            default -> \"Unknown\";\n        };\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(\"REST Port: \" + resolveDefaultPort(GatewayProtocol.HTTP_REST));\n        System.out.println(\"Kafka Port: \" + resolveDefaultPort(GatewayProtocol.KAFKA_STREAM));\n        System.out.println(\"Day 1: \" + classifyDayModern(1));\n        System.out.println(\"Day 6: \" + classifyDayModern(6));\n        System.out.println(\"Day 99: \" + classifyDayModern(99));\n    }\n}",
