              "title": "Method Overloading: Compile-Time Signature Resolution, Widening & Boxing Rules",
              "content": "In API design, methods frequently need to perform the same semantic operation across different input types. For example, a logging framework must support logging Strings, integers, booleans, and arbitrary domain objects. Forcing developers to remember distinct method names (such as 'logString', 'logInt', 'logObject') would create unnecessary cognitive load and ugly API surfaces. Method Overloading allows a class to declare multiple methods that share the exact same method identifier, provided each method declares a distinct parameter list (different parameter count, different parameter types, or a different ordering of types).\n\nMethod overloading is an example of Static Polymorphism (or Compile-Time Polymorphism). The critical architectural invariant of method overloading is that method resolution occurs strictly at compile time based on the static, declared types of the arguments! When javac compiles a method invocation, it inspects the argument types provided by the caller, searches the class for candidate methods matching the method identifier, and selects the 'most specific' applicable method signature. Once selected, javac hard-codes the target signature directly into the generated bytecode (via the invokevirtual or invokestatic instruction). At runtime, the JVM executes that pre-selected signature directly without dynamic type inspection overhead.\n\nWhen resolving overloaded methods with primitive and wrapper arguments, the Java compiler follows a strict 3-phase precedence protocol established by the Java Language Specification (JLS \u00a715.12.2):\n1. Phase 1: Subtyping and Primitive Widening without Boxing (e.g., widening an 'int' argument to match 'long', 'float', or 'double').\n2. Phase 2: Autoboxing and Unboxing Conversions (e.g., boxing primitive 'int' to 'Integer', or unboxing 'Double' to primitive 'double').\n3. Phase 3: Variable Arity (varargs) Invocations (e.g., matching 'int... numbers').\n\nCrucially, the compiler will never perform primitive widening followed by autoboxing in a single step! For example, an 'int' cannot be widened to 'long' and then boxed to 'Long' (an int can box to Integer, but not to Long). If multiple candidate methods are equally applicable without one being strictly more specific than the other, the compiler rejects the invocation with an 'ambiguous method call' compilation error. Understanding this compiler resolution hierarchy allows engineers to design intuitive, predictable enterprise APIs without surprising method dispatch traps. Furthermore, consider how method overloading interacts with inheritance and the null literal. If an overloaded method declares both 'print(String s)' and 'print(Object o)', and a caller passes 'print(null)', which method executes? Following JLS \u00a715.12.2.5, javac searches for the most specific method: because java.lang.String is a subtype of java.lang.Object, the String parameter is strictly more specific, so 'print(String s)' is chosen! However, if the class declares 'print(String s)' and 'print(Integer i)', passing 'print(null)' produces a compile-time ambiguity error because neither String nor Integer is a subtype of the other, leaving the compiler with no mathematical basis for preference. In enterprise frameworks, developers avoid overloading methods with identical parameter counts where arguments are closely related in class hierarchies, preferring distinct method names (like 'formatInteger' vs 'formatDecimal') to guarantee clear, unambiguous calling contracts for consumer teams. In addition, when designing library APIs, static analysis tools such as SpotBugs and SonarQube flag overloaded methods that rely on subtle widening or boxing rules as code smells, recommending that engineers provide explicit, distinct method names to eliminate any potential developer ambiguity or maintenance confusion during future code refactoring cycles. This rigorous API governance guarantees that shared common libraries remain intuitive and resilient against accidental client misuse across large global engineering organizations, preventing costly regressions in mission-critical customer production workflows across cloud environments. In the interactive lab below, we trace compiler overload resolution across widening, boxing, and reference hierarchies.",
              "code": "public class MethodOverloadingLab {\n    // Overloaded variants for a financial formatting service\n    public static String formatAmount(int amount) {\n        return \"Whole Currency: $\" + amount + \".00\";\n    }\n\n    public static String formatAmount(double amount) {\n        return String.format(\"Precise Currency: $%.2f\", amount);\n    }\n\n    public static String formatAmount(double amount, String currencyCode) {\n        return String.format(\"%s $%.2f\", currencyCode, amount);\n    }\n\n    // Demonstrating compiler resolution precedence: Widening vs Boxing\n    public static void testResolution(long val) {\n        System.out.println(\"  -> Dispatched to primitive widening: testResolution(long)\");\n    }\n\n    public static void testResolution(Integer val) {\n        System.out.println(\"  -> Dispatched to autoboxing: testResolution(Integer)\");\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(formatAmount(50));                // Resolves to formatAmount(int)\n        System.out.println(formatAmount(49.99));             // Resolves to formatAmount(double)\n        System.out.println(formatAmount(120.50, \"EUR\"));     // Resolves to formatAmount(double, String)\n\n        System.out.println(\"\nTesting Compiler Precedence with 'int' literal 42:\");\n        // JLS Rule: Primitive widening takes precedence over Autoboxing!\n        testResolution(42); // Matches testResolution(long) rather than testResolution(Integer)!\n    }\n}",
