              "title": "Capstone Lab: Production-Grade Matrix Diagonal Calculator & Geometric Ledger",
              "content": "We culminate Day 14 by synthesizing our mastery of 2D array heap representation, square matrix properties, boundary validation guards, and O(N) linear loop optimization into our Day 14 Challenge Capstone: The Production-Grade Matrix Diagonal Calculator.\n\nIn numerical software platforms (such as linear algebra solvers, 3D graphics transformation pipelines, and financial ledger correlation matrices), calculating diagonal sums is a foundational computational primitive. In our canonical Solution class, we author 'sumDiagonal(int[][] matrix)' to satisfy all production software invariants:\n1. Defensive Null & Empty Verification: If a caller passes null, the method rejects it immediately with an informative exception. If a caller passes an empty matrix (length 0), the method returns 0 deterministically.\n2. O(N) Linear Time Efficiency: The algorithm employs a single loop ('for int i = 0; i < matrix.length; i++'), directly accumulating 'matrix[i][i]' in O(N) time and O(1) space with zero wasted iterations.\n3. Complete Test Suite Coverage: The method is validated against diverse test cases: 3x3 matrices, 2x2 matrices, 1x1 single-element matrices, and matrices containing mixed negative and positive values.\n\nFrom an automated software testing perspective, the sumDiagonal method must be validated across a comprehensive suite of edge-case scenarios: standard 3x3 matrices with positive numbers, 2x2 matrices containing negative values, single-element 1x1 matrices, and empty matrices. Automated unit tests must also verify that passing null triggers an immediate, well-documented IllegalArgumentException. In high-performance scientific computing and enterprise financial backends, writing clean, branch-free algorithmic kernels that leverage mathematical invariants is the hallmark of senior software engineering craftsmanship. By implementing this O(N) linear diagonal summation algorithm, our Solution class sets an exemplary standard of computational efficiency and defensive resilience that prepares us for advanced search algorithms on Day 15. Furthermore, when implementing numerical algorithms in high-reliability financial backends, writing clean, pure functions that operate on multi-dimensional matrices guarantees that data ledgers remain immutable and audit trails remain pristine. By adhering to uncompromising defensive validation and optimal linear complexity, our Solution class embodies the highest standards of professional enterprise Java craftsmanship and engineering excellence. In the interactive lab below, we trace the complete production Solution class, demonstrating clean defensive validation, optimal O(N) execution, and automated test suite verification. Moreover, when writing unit tests for matrix operations in enterprise CI/CD deployment pipelines, automated test suites should include property-based tests that verify mathematical invariants across thousands of generated matrices: verifying that reversing the matrix rows inverts the primary and secondary diagonal sums predictably, confirming that scalar multiplication of the matrix scales the diagonal sum proportionally, and asserting that all negative values accumulate without arithmetic overflow. This level of uncompromising unit testing discipline ensures that billing infrastructure behaves flawlessly across billions of dollars in processed commercial transactions. In addition, when implementing geometric engines in cloud-native environments, stateless pure calculation methods allow diagonal calculations to scale horizontally across serverless workers or distributed Kubernetes pods with zero synchronization overhead, delivering reliable sub-millisecond latencies to end users worldwide. In conclusion, by mastering multi-dimensional array representation, square matrix invariants, defensive boundary guards, and optimal O(N) linear single-loop traversals here on Day 14, you possess the full analytical toolkit needed to process complex data planes, setting the stage for our Milestone 3 data ledger and binary search algorithms on Day 15. Furthermore, when implementing numerical algorithms in high-reliability financial backends, writing clean, pure functions that operate on multi-dimensional matrices guarantees that data ledgers remain immutable and audit trails remain pristine. By adhering to uncompromising defensive validation and optimal linear complexity, our Solution class embodies the highest standards of professional enterprise Java craftsmanship and engineering excellence.",
              "code": "public class Solution {\n    /**\n     * Calculates the sum of elements along the main diagonal of a square matrix.\n     * Uses an optimal O(N) single-loop traversal.\n     *\n     * @param matrix 2D integer square matrix\n     * @return Sum of elements along the main diagonal (matrix[i][i])\n     * @throws IllegalArgumentException if matrix is null\n     */\n    public static int sumDiagonal(int[][] matrix) {\n        // Defensive Precondition Guard\n        if (matrix == null) {\n            throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Matrix reference cannot be null.\");\n        }\n\n        // Empty matrix returns 0 deterministically\n        if (matrix.length == 0) {\n            return 0;\n        }\n\n        int diagonalSum = 0;\n        int n = matrix.length;\n\n        // Optimal O(N) single-loop traversal:\n        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {\n            diagonalSum += matrix[i][i];\n        }\n\n        return diagonalSum;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(\"=== DAY 14 CHALLENGE: MATRIX DIAGONAL SUM ===\");\n\n        // Test 1: Standard 3x3 Matrix\n        int[][] matrix3x3 = {\n            { 1, 2, 3 },\n            { 4, 5, 6 },\n            { 7, 8, 9 }\n        };\n        // Diagonal: 1 + 5 + 9 = 15\n        System.out.println(\"3x3 Matrix Diagonal Sum: \" + sumDiagonal(matrix3x3)); // Expected: 15\n\n        // Test 2: 2x2 Matrix with negative numbers\n        int[][] matrix2x2 = {\n            { 10, -3 },\n            { 4,  20 }\n        };\n        // Diagonal: 10 + 20 = 30\n        System.out.println(\"2x2 Matrix Diagonal Sum: \" + sumDiagonal(matrix2x2)); // Expected: 30\n\n        // Test 3: 1x1 Matrix (Single element)\n        int[][] matrix1x1 = { { 42 } };\n        System.out.println(\"1x1 Matrix Diagonal Sum: \" + sumDiagonal(matrix1x1)); // Expected: 42\n\n        // Test 4: Empty Matrix\n        int[][] emptyMatrix = {};\n        System.out.println(\"Empty Matrix Diagonal Sum: \" + sumDiagonal(emptyMatrix)); // Expected: 0\n    }\n}",
